COPY strapi.contributors (id, document_id, first_name, last_name, role, affiliation, description, created_at, updated_at, published_at, created_by_id, updated_by_id, locale) FROM stdin;
129	pihr5zimloltxxntfnfdga1q	Pascal	Taranto	Specialist on John Stuart Mill​	Aix-Marseille Université 	Pascal Taranto is a French philosopher and academic, known for his expertise in modern philosophy and ethics. His work often explores the intersections of philosophy, literature, and society. Taranto’s insights into John Stuart Mill’s philosophy contribute significantly to our understanding of utilitarianism and liberal thought.​	2025-10-05 08:14:13.229	2025-10-05 08:14:13.229	\N	\N	\N	en
130	pihr5zimloltxxntfnfdga1q	Pascal	Taranto	Specialist on John Stuart Mill​	Aix-Marseille Université 	Pascal Taranto is a French philosopher and academic, known for his expertise in modern philosophy and ethics. His work often explores the intersections of philosophy, literature, and society. Taranto’s insights into John Stuart Mill’s philosophy contribute significantly to our understanding of utilitarianism and liberal thought.​	2025-10-05 08:14:13.229	2025-10-05 08:14:13.229	2025-10-05 08:14:13.242	\N	\N	en
131	mn5xos15jkcamgf49r71w5sx	Owen	Goldin	Specialist on Aristotle​	Marquette University	Owen Goldin specializes in ancient philosophy. He authored Explaining an Eclipse and translated works by Philoponus on Aristotle’s Posterior Analytics. His research spans the Presocratics, Plato, Aristotle, Hellenistic thought, Jewish philosophy, metaphysics, and social philosophy. He teaches both graduate and undergraduate courses in ancient philosophy.	2025-10-05 08:16:27.579	2025-10-05 08:16:27.579	\N	\N	\N	en
132	mn5xos15jkcamgf49r71w5sx	Owen	Goldin	Specialist on Aristotle​	Marquette University	Owen Goldin specializes in ancient philosophy. He authored Explaining an Eclipse and translated works by Philoponus on Aristotle’s Posterior Analytics. His research spans the Presocratics, Plato, Aristotle, Hellenistic thought, Jewish philosophy, metaphysics, and social philosophy. He teaches both graduate and undergraduate courses in ancient philosophy.	2025-10-05 08:16:27.579	2025-10-05 08:16:27.579	2025-10-05 08:16:27.593	\N	\N	en
134	vimnprmq5rfj594dmgzcd67f	Allen	Wood	Specialist on Immanuel Kant	Indiana University	Professor Allen W. Wood is the Ruth Norman Halls Professor of Philosophy at Indiana University. He is a leading scholar on Immanuel Kant, particularly in ethics and political philosophy. His extensive work explores Kantian moral theory, German idealism, and contemporary ethical debates.	2025-11-08 08:26:48.053	2025-11-08 08:26:48.053	2025-11-08 08:26:48.098	\N	\N	en
135	e6uc021tj5jf3sghkikx521l	Joshua	Forstenzer	Specialist on John Dewey	University of Sheffield	Senior Lecturer in Philosophy and Co-Director of the Centre for Engaged Philosophy. Joshua works mostly on John Dewey, the tradition of American pragmatism, democratic education, and other related topics. His work has been supported by the British Academy, the Yale Centre for Faith and Culture, and Higher Education Innovation Funding.	2025-11-08 08:28:27.771	2025-11-08 08:28:27.771	\N	\N	\N	en
136	e6uc021tj5jf3sghkikx521l	Joshua	Forstenzer	Specialist on John Dewey	University of Sheffield	Senior Lecturer in Philosophy and Co-Director of the Centre for Engaged Philosophy. Joshua works mostly on John Dewey, the tradition of American pragmatism, democratic education, and other related topics. His work has been supported by the British Academy, the Yale Centre for Faith and Culture, and Higher Education Innovation Funding.	2025-11-08 08:28:27.771	2025-11-08 08:28:27.771	2025-11-08 08:28:27.788	\N	\N	en
137	g01r0nragm6rr4dp5l9vsevj	Melissa	Yates	Specialist on Habermas	Minerva University	Melissa Yates is a professor of moral and political philosophy. She earned her Ph.D. in philosophy at Northwestern University researching theories of public reasoning developed by John Rawls and Jürgen Habermas. Yates’ research addresses social, political, and ethical philosophical questions about the ways citizens exercise power, and the institutions that constrain, enable and coerce citizen engagement in public life. In her manuscript in progress, Democracy as Strangers: Governing without Ties of Intimacy, she argues that current democratic theory fails to account for one of the most important realities of contemporary democratic life, namely the fact that the vast majority of democratic citizens are fundamentally strangers to one another.	2025-11-08 08:29:21.867	2025-11-08 08:29:21.867	\N	\N	\N	en
138	g01r0nragm6rr4dp5l9vsevj	Melissa	Yates	Specialist on Habermas	Minerva University	Melissa Yates is a professor of moral and political philosophy. She earned her Ph.D. in philosophy at Northwestern University researching theories of public reasoning developed by John Rawls and Jürgen Habermas. Yates’ research addresses social, political, and ethical philosophical questions about the ways citizens exercise power, and the institutions that constrain, enable and coerce citizen engagement in public life. In her manuscript in progress, Democracy as Strangers: Governing without Ties of Intimacy, she argues that current democratic theory fails to account for one of the most important realities of contemporary democratic life, namely the fact that the vast majority of democratic citizens are fundamentally strangers to one another.	2025-11-08 08:29:21.867	2025-11-08 08:29:21.867	2025-11-08 08:29:21.881	\N	\N	en
133	vimnprmq5rfj594dmgzcd67f	Allen	Wood	Specialist on Immanuel Kant	Indiana University	Professor Allen W. Wood is the Ruth Norman Halls Professor of Philosophy at Indiana University. He is a leading scholar on Immanuel Kant, particularly in ethics and political philosophy. His extensive work explores Kantian moral theory, German idealism, and contemporary ethical debates.	2025-11-08 08:26:48.053	2025-11-08 08:26:48.053	\N	\N	\N	en
127	o6hfuuk583kac46464ghzpa3	Kate	Cooper	Specialist on Augustine	University of London	Kate Cooper is a renowned historian and author, specializing in the study of women and religion in late antiquity. Her recent work, Queens of a Fallen World: The Lost Women of Augustine’s Confessions, delves into the lives of four influential women in Augustine’s early life: his mother Monnica, his lover, his fiancée, and Empress Justina. Cooper’s research offers a fresh perspective on Augustine’s Confessions, highlighting the significant impact these women had on his life and writings.​	2025-09-29 16:11:05.663	2025-10-05 08:12:53.389	\N	\N	\N	en
128	o6hfuuk583kac46464ghzpa3	Kate	Cooper	Specialist on Augustine	University of London	Kate Cooper is a renowned historian and author, specializing in the study of women and religion in late antiquity. Her recent work, Queens of a Fallen World: The Lost Women of Augustine’s Confessions, delves into the lives of four influential women in Augustine’s early life: his mother Monnica, his lover, his fiancée, and Empress Justina. Cooper’s research offers a fresh perspective on Augustine’s Confessions, highlighting the significant impact these women had on his life and writings.​	2025-09-29 16:11:05.663	2025-10-05 08:12:53.389	2025-10-05 08:12:53.412	\N	\N	en
139	uwa9gyxpll9kdyci9q61pif9	Stephen	Angle	Specialist on Confucius	Wesleyan University	A philosophy writer and researcher specializing in Chinese Philosophy, Confucianism, Neo-Confucianism, and comparative philosophy, Stephen C. Angle’s research focuses on philosophy’s role in human rights, politics, and ethics both in China and globally. Fluent in Mandarin and in classical Chinese, Angle has spent Fulbright years in Taipei and in Beijing, and was a Berggruen Fellow at Tsinghua University during academic year 2016-17. Many of his books and essays have appeared in Chinese translation.	2025-11-08 08:30:09.181	2025-11-08 08:30:09.181	\N	\N	\N	en
140	uwa9gyxpll9kdyci9q61pif9	Stephen	Angle	Specialist on Confucius	Wesleyan University	A philosophy writer and researcher specializing in Chinese Philosophy, Confucianism, Neo-Confucianism, and comparative philosophy, Stephen C. Angle’s research focuses on philosophy’s role in human rights, politics, and ethics both in China and globally. Fluent in Mandarin and in classical Chinese, Angle has spent Fulbright years in Taipei and in Beijing, and was a Berggruen Fellow at Tsinghua University during academic year 2016-17. Many of his books and essays have appeared in Chinese translation.	2025-11-08 08:30:09.181	2025-11-08 08:30:09.181	2025-11-08 08:30:09.198	\N	\N	en
